import{_ as t,o as s,c as r,a0 as a}from"./chunks/framework.D4fUSYzX.js";const m=JSON.parse(`{"title":"ADR 0152: The merged agent reader reads the held terminals in one batch, and the observation cycle's period holds its interval","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/0152-the-merged-agent-reader-reads-the-held-terminals-in-one-batch-and-the-observation-cycles-period-holds-its-interval.md","filePath":"adr/0152-the-merged-agent-reader-reads-the-held-terminals-in-one-batch-and-the-observation-cycles-period-holds-its-interval.md"}`),o={name:"adr/0152-the-merged-agent-reader-reads-the-held-terminals-in-one-batch-and-the-observation-cycles-period-holds-its-interval.md"};function n(h,e,i,d,l,c){return s(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="adr-0152-the-merged-agent-reader-reads-the-held-terminals-in-one-batch-and-the-observation-cycle-s-period-holds-its-interval" tabindex="-1">ADR 0152: The merged agent reader reads the held terminals in one batch, and the observation cycle&#39;s period holds its interval <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0152-the-merged-agent-reader-reads-the-held-terminals-in-one-batch-and-the-observation-cycle-s-period-holds-its-interval" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;ADR 0152: The merged agent reader reads the held terminals in one batch, and the observation cycle&#39;s period holds its interval&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Status: accepted Date: 2026-11-14</p><p>Records that the merged agent reader&#39;s probe reads the plane&#39;s held terminal rows in one concurrent batch: the roster read stands, and the record reads of the roster&#39;s held rows run together, after the roster settles, instead of one behind the next, on the herdr-core host where those reads are the probe&#39;s per-terminal cost. And records that the observation coordinator schedules the next cycle at the previous cycle&#39;s start plus the interval, clamped to now when that instant is in the past, so the cycle&#39;s period holds its interval even when a cycle runs long. The Agent reader&#39;s interface, the Agent wait&#39;s wake, and the cycle&#39;s one report move none of them.</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Context&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The operator watches the tickets, and the machine works them. The Observation cycle is the plane&#39;s watch over the machine: on each period it probes the Agents the host lists, settles the turns the probe shows ended, fires their Transitions, and runs the Pickup that starts what the settled turns freed.</p><p>On the herdr host the probe is the one <code>herdr agent list</code> call ADR 0006 decided: one process carries every Agent, and the probe&#39;s cost stands off the size of the in-flight set. On the herdr-core host the merged agent reader that stands on the run&#39;s resolved host (ADR 0142) pays more: the roster there carries no status word, so the probe runs one unscoped roster read and then one record read per held terminal. The held terminals are bounded to the plane&#39;s own: the recorded names of its in-flight Tickets and Consultations on that host, read from the state&#39;s handoff facts, so the poll never reads every terminal on the machine. Today those record reads run one behind the next, and the probe&#39;s cost grows with the in-flight set, one process after the last.</p><p>The coordinator owns the loop&#39;s period, and it schedules the next cycle after the previous one completes: the timer stands on the cycle&#39;s end, so the real period is the interval plus the cycle&#39;s cost, the interval defaulting to five seconds. On the herdr-core host the two costs meet and reinforce each other: the slowest cycle is the one with the most Agents in flight, it is the probe that reads the most terminals in a row, and it is the cycle that delays the next probe the longest. The machine&#39;s busiest moment is the moment the plane looks least often.</p><p>The cost lands where the operator reads it. An Agent that finished a while ago still shows as working, because the probe that would settle it waited behind the sequential reads and behind the period the overrun stole. A slot just freed by a finished Agent stays unfilled for an extra period, because the Pickup that would fill it runs on the late probe. And a terminal the roster stops listing is corrected on the next probe: the Startup grace holds the automatic Restart, and nothing moves the displayed state before that probe. ADR 0084&#39;s Agent wait already carries the state-change latency: one wait per in-flight working Agent, and the state match runs a cycle now. The period&#39;s cost therefore shows only on what the wait does not watch: the Missing agent&#39;s correction, and the staleness of the facts the wait holds no wake over.</p><p>The depth is shallow on both sides, and both sides stand in the modules that already own the facts. The record reads are independent of one another: each stands on its roster row and on no other record read, so the serialization is a choice of the implementation, not a fact of the work. The schedule rule stands on the coordinator&#39;s own clock, beside the interval it already reads each cycle. Neither deepening moves the Agent reader&#39;s interface: the same rows, in the roster&#39;s order, the same failure semantics. The reader keeps its one seam to the outside: the process spawning already runs through the command runner, and a test counts the runner&#39;s commands.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decision&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p>The probe&#39;s core half reads all of the plane&#39;s held terminal rows in one concurrent batch. The roster read stands, and when it settles, the record reads of the roster&#39;s held rows are issued together and awaited together, instead of one behind the next. On the herdr-core host, which the run&#39;s host resolution names (ADR 0141, ADR 0142, ADR 0143, ADR 0144, ADR 0148), that half is the probe&#39;s per-terminal cost. On the herdr host the probe is the one list call that carries every Agent, and the half reads the terminals the run&#39;s own handoffs put on herdr-core, empty when none, so the batch is a no-op where the probe is one call.</p></li><li><p>The Agent reader&#39;s interface moves nothing. The merged list carries the same rows, in the roster&#39;s order, and the failure semantics stand exactly as the reader reports them today: a roster the host does not answer is the probe&#39;s error on the herdr-core host, where the observation holds, and the stated gap beside the standing herdr half on the herdr host; a record that does not answer leaves the terminal present with an unknown status; a terminal the host lost drops its row, so the Missing marker reads it and no settled turn does.</p></li><li><p>The coordinator schedules the next cycle at the previous cycle&#39;s start plus the interval, clamped to now when that instant is in the past, instead of at the previous cycle&#39;s end plus the interval. A probe that finishes early holds the period at exactly the interval, and a probe that runs past its slot runs the next cycle at the slow cycle&#39;s completion: the grid re-anchors on that cycle&#39;s own start, and the period returns to the interval the moment the probe keeps the pace again. A timer fire that meets a cycle already in flight runs no cycle of its own: it holds that in-flight cycle&#39;s start as the grid anchor.</p></li><li><p>The first tick stands: the boot runs the first cycle immediately, and its next cycle stands at the boot&#39;s start plus the interval, the way it does today. The interval stands as it stands today: the loop policy&#39;s named interval where the policy names one, the config&#39;s poll setting otherwise, derived each cycle.</p></li><li><p>ADR 0084&#39;s Agent wait moves nothing: the state match still runs a cycle now, beside the timer&#39;s grid, and the poll stays the single source of truth.</p></li></ul><h2 id="considered-options" tabindex="-1">Considered options <a class="header-anchor" href="#considered-options" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Considered options&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Keep the sequential reads and schedule from the completion (status quo): the probe&#39;s cost grows with the in-flight set, one process after the last, and every late cycle steals its overrun from the next probe, so the machine&#39;s busiest moment is the moment the plane looks least often.</li><li>Hold the period and keep the sequential reads: the period stops compounding, but the slowest cycle still pays the whole sequential run, and a run of long cycles now runs back to back, each paying that same run, so the operator&#39;s view of the machine lags by the whole run instead of by one process.</li><li>Batch the reads and keep scheduling from the completion: the probe gets faster, but the period still stands on the probe&#39;s end, so a slow settle or a slow walk still delays the next probe by its overrun.</li><li>Read each held terminal on its own cadence: it scatters the probe&#39;s one observation into many independent reads, breaks the standing ADR 0006 set so one call carries every Agent and the state transitions and the Parallel limit&#39;s count share one observation, and multiplies the processes the runner spawns.</li><li>Ask the roster to carry the status words: the root cause of the per-terminal reads is the herdr-core roster&#39;s missing status word, and asking the host for one is a change on the host&#39;s side of the seam. The plane reads the records the host offers, and the batch makes those records cost the slowest, not the sum.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Consequences&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>The operator: on the herdr-core host, a finished Agent shows as settled on the next probe whatever the size of the in-flight set, a just-freed slot is filled by the Pickup on the next probe instead of the probe after, and a terminal the roster stopped listing is read as a Missing agent on the first probe after it left. On the herdr host the operator sees no change: the probe is the one list call it always was.</li><li>The machine: the herdr-core probe pays the slowest of the concurrent record reads, not their sum, and the period holds its interval: a slow probe steals no time from the next probe, and the grid re-anchors at the pace the probe keeps. The Agent wait still runs a cycle now on the state match, and the poll stays the single source of truth.</li><li>The test: the coordinator seam, the observation suite that drives the real coordinator end to end over the fake command runner on the virtual clock, pins the batch: one probe issues the roster read and the held terminal reads, the runner&#39;s command list counts them, the batch issues after the roster settles, and the rows come back in the roster&#39;s order. The suite pins the period on the virtual clock: a cycle longer than the interval runs its next cycle at its own completion, a shorter one at exactly start plus interval. The wake&#39;s pin still runs a cycle now on the state match, and the herdr host&#39;s one-list-call pin and the Missing agent&#39;s flow pins stand unchanged.</li><li>This ADR is the deepening ADR 0151 named as separate: the herdr side of the work, one process per terminal the plane polls (the review&#39;s O2). The refresh a settle takes when the turn&#39;s source gives nothing (the review&#39;s O3) stays separate, and the sibling record ADR 0153 scopes it. These records land with the implementation, not with this ADR.</li></ul>',15)])])}const u=t(o,[["render",n]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
